Z||000000||88formula||04-23-2000||10:23 PM||||I was thinking of getting the fuel pressure regulator the fiero store sells. There is one thing though that I don't understand. If the regulator is so far under the upper intake manifold than how do you adjust the thing? The fiero people here that have it what do you do?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric||04-23-2000||10:28 PM||||I have to take a few plug wires off the cap, but otherwise it's not too bad.  Most of the adjustments are small and you have to take your time.  When I bought my regulator, I didn't know that using a coin like a quater or a couple of quarters would do the same thing.  I'd try that first, if I was you.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ironhorse||04-23-2000||11:03 PM||||I have ABSOLUTILY(SP) NOOOOOOO IDEEEEEEAAAA what the purpose of having a fuel regulator, what they are use for, and for what setting or whatever else you set it for.... gimme the site that will explain it or let me know in here.... I feel kinda stupid cuz everybody talking about fuel regulator (outside of forums) and I sit there nodding like I know what it is use for and how to use it :P||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||04-23-2000||11:16 PM||||It does exactly what it says. It regulators your fuel pressure.  Meaning you can increase and decrease the amount of fuel that's dumped into you motor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-23-2000||11:33 PM||||The fuel injector only opens for a specific amount of time. In order to get more fuel you would increase fuel pressure. If pressure is too high engine floods. Too low engine doesn't run. The regulator maintains a set pressure level in the fuel rail. If you modify your engine, like rebuild it to a High output 2.8L, 3.1L, 3.2L or change to a 3.4L you would need to increase fuel flow so an adjustable fuel pressure regulator can help you fine tune your fuel delivery for the maximum power and/or efficency. You can also adjust your stock 2.8L for better fuel delivery too. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||04-24-2000||04:41 PM||||Eric, How much did you increase you fuel pressure for the modifications you have done (list mods so that I have some perspective)? <P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||04-25-2000||03:16 AM||||I've heard about the .25$ fuel pressure reg. mod.  Anybody have instructions?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||04-25-2000||10:34 AM||||My fuel pressure was like 37 psi before I put the regulator on it.  I've had it up to 50, but it seems to run better with around 45 psi or so.  I also have an Autometer air/fuel ratio gauge which helps tell you if you have enough fuel at wot (should read a little rich).  My car has a 3.1 rebuild with the usual mods such as porting heads, manifolds, cam.  True horsepower gains were marginal, I probably only have 150hp.  The pistons I use Fed Mogul H562CP had quite a big dish and lowered compression to less than 8.0:1.  This car needs a turbo!||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||04-25-2000||11:28 PM||||Yeah, cranking up the fuel pressure doesn't always have the intended effect (affect?). The injectors tend to spit above 50 PSI.||||reg||1
